    "4": {
      "brokenCode": "int i = 0, j = 0, k = l;\nwhile (i < n1 && j < n2) {\n    if (L[i] <= R[j]) arr[k++] = L[i++];\n    else arr[k++] = R[j++];\n}\n// BUG: missing the two leftover-copying while loops entirely",
      "bugDescription": "Once one of L or R is fully consumed, the main merge loop exits — but without the two follow-up while loops copying any remaining elements from whichever side wasn't exhausted, those trailing elements are simply never written back into arr, leaving stale or garbage values in the tail of the merged range.",
      "mascotDialogue": "It's easy to think the merge is 'done' once the main comparison loop ends — but one side almost always finishes before the other, and forgetting to drain the leftovers loses real data, silently."
    },
    "5": {
      "challengeType": "multiple-choice",
      "question": "Is merge sort a stable sorting algorithm (does it preserve the relative order of equal elements)?",
      "answer": "Yes, as long as the merge step takes from the left half on ties (L[i] <= R[j])",
      "hints": [
        "Look at the comparison operator in the merge step.",
        "Stability depends on which side wins when elements are equal."
      ],
      "solution": "Yes — because the merge condition uses <= and always favors the left half on a tie, elements that were equal and originally on the left retain their relative order in the output."
    }
